# Rebuildable or machine-specific; never versioned.
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/

# PyInstaller output. dist\Bookkeeping.exe is fully regenerable from the tracked
# sources with build.bat, so it is not versioned -- but note that means Git
# cannot restore a working binary either. build.bat keeps the previous .exe as
# dist\Bookkeeping.previous.exe for exactly that reason.
build/
dist/

# The user's own books, receipt images, log and API key. Personal data, not source.
data/
sample-receipt.png

# Receipt photographs dropped in the project folder while testing. A real
# receipt is somebody's shopping, their payment method and often their address,
# so none of them belong in a published repository. This is why the real receipt
# is kept in the tests as OCR word boxes and a transcription
# (tests/fixtures/walmart_ocr_words.json, tests/test_real_receipt.py) rather than
# as the photograph itself -- the tests get the same regression cover, and
# nothing personal is published.
# Unanchored on purpose: `tools\screenshot_pages.py` and `verify_exe.py --out`
# both write PNGs into a subdirectory (`shots\` by default), which is exactly
# where a root-only rule would stop protecting anything.
# Receipt photographs. **Describes a hole that is now closed** -- kept rather
# than deleted because the reason the extension list has to be generous is
# easier to understand from the gap it used to have than from the list itself.
# Until the rules below were extended, they caught receipts only because today's
# test files happen to be .jpg: one saved as .heic (the iPhone default), .webp,
# or a scanned .pdf was untracked *and* unignored, and the next `git add -A`
# would have published somebody's shopping and card digits. See the next comment
# block for what is covered now.
pictures/

*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.jfif
*.png
# The formats the comment above named as the hole, now actually covered. A phone
# or a scanner does not produce .jpg just because today's test photographs did:
# .heic is the iPhone default, .jfif is what Chrome on Windows saves a JPEG as,
# .webp and .avif come out of a browser download, .bmp/.tif/.tiff are scanner
# output, and a scanned receipt is very often a .pdf. Every one of them would
# have been untracked *and* unignored, so a single `git add -A` would have
# published somebody's shopping. None is tracked anywhere here, so they cost
# nothing.
#
# Two limits, worth knowing now rather than discovering later:
#
# * **These patterns are case-sensitive where the filesystem is.** They catch
#   `IMG_0001.HEIC` on this machine only because NTFS gives Git
#   core.ignorecase=true -- and upper case is exactly what an iPhone emits. On a
#   Linux clone, or a case-sensitive macOS volume, that filename is unignored
#   again. Run `git check-ignore` there rather than assuming this file travels.
# * **A `!` line can only re-include a file that has no excluded parent
#   DIRECTORY.** `!docs/something.pdf` would work; nothing can rescue a file
#   inside `pictures/` or `data/`, which is precisely where a receipt PDF would
#   land. Treat that as the intended shape rather than a defect: those two
#   directories stay absolutely closed, and anything meant to be published
#   belongs outside them.
*.heic
*.heif
*.webp
*.avif
*.bmp
*.tif
*.tiff
*.pdf
!assets/icon-preview.png
# The README's screenshots. Every one was taken with
# `tools\screenshot_pages.py --tight` against demo books seeded by
# `tools\seed_demo.py` -- never against real books. Without --tight the capture
# box reaches outside the window and picks up whatever is behind it.
#
# receipts.png is REDACTED after capture: the Payment field is blurred. Re-shoot
# it and the blur is gone, so redact again before committing. The value under it
# is demo data from seed_demo.py and not anybody's card, but a card-shaped string
# beside real merchant names reads as real to a stranger who cannot audit the
# fixture, and a published image is judged on how it looks, not on where it came
# from.
#
# Named one by one rather than as `!docs/screenshots/*.png`, which is the
# obvious way to write it and is worse. A directory pattern re-includes any PNG
# later dropped in that folder, and in a project whose entire image policy
# exists because real receipt photographs land in the working tree, that is the
# one place a receipt could become publishable without anybody editing this
# file. An allowlist of four names cannot do that: a fifth image has to be added
# here on purpose, which is the moment somebody should be thinking about it.
!docs/screenshots/receipts.png
!docs/screenshots/reports.png
!docs/screenshots/rules.png
!docs/screenshots/settings.png
